"Logical Reasoning"], "rationale": "Convert community accounts into accurate registers, reconcile stakeholder needs and track project actions. These are draft importance estimates for role requirements, not learner aptitude scores; SME review required."}, "big5_assessment": {"traits": ["Agreeableness", "Conscientiousness", "Extraversion"], "rationale": "Clear communication and dependable follow-up support coordination without making unverified assurances. Trait-demand ratings are provisional role descriptors, not personality diagnosis or exclusion criteria."}, "work_values_assessment": {"values": ["Impact", "Leadership", "Autonomy"], "rationale": "Community impact and cooperative project work are central; accountability stays with the authorised project lead. Work-value ratings are provisional career-exploration descriptors, not employment or pay guarantees."}}, "embedding_text": "ROLE\nName: Water Resources / WASH Project Associate\n\nCAREER CONTEXT\nIndustry: Water Resources, Environment and Public Utilities\nDomain: Environmental Compliance and Public Programmes\nRole Family: Water Resources and WASH Project Support\n\nROLE SUMMARY\nWater Resources / WASH Project Associate supports coordinate community-facing water programmes in the RAREMINDS River Mission workplace case. May support programme coordination and consultations; cannot certify health claims, release sampling teams, approve budgets or speak as a public authority.\n\nWORK CONTEXT\nL1 community and site evidence → L4 stakeholder/project actions → L5 WASH-route portfolio. Inputs: complaint records, site/stakeholder map, household water-use accounts, monitoring schedule and community activity logs. Course-wide foundations: CAP01, CAP02, CAP06, CAP07, CAP08, CAP11. Role focus: CAP02, CAP03, CAP10. May support programme coordination and consultations; cannot certify health claims, release sampling teams, approve budgets or speak as a public authority.\n\nTYPICAL WORK ACTIVITIES\nWater Resources / WASH Project Associate: Document affected households and water uses without double counting; Coordinate field and stakeholder information with the assigned team; Maintain activity, issue, owner and progress registers; Draft evidence-based programme updates with unresolved needs. When source evidence is missing, record a HOLD/exception and identify the reviewing owner.\n\nKEY RESPONSIBILITIES & ACTIONS\nDocument affected households and water uses without double counting; Coordinate field and stakeholder information with the assigned team; Maintain activity, issue, owner and progress registers; Draft evidence-based programme updates with unresolved needs; Check each saved record against source ID, units, errors and role authority.\n\nDELIVERABLES & OUTPUTS\nSource-backed outputs: Stakeholder and water-use register; Community consultation and issue log; Water project progress and responsibility tracker; Supervised programme handover and feedback note. Associated capability evidence: CAP01 Investigation brief; CAP02 Stakeholder and site register; CAP03 Monitoring plan; CAP06 Validated analytical workbook; CAP07 Evidence and hypothesis matrix; CAP08 Criteria comparison and gap report; CAP10 Project action pack; CAP11 Final professional investigation portfolio.\n\nINTEREST AND WORK-STYLE ALIGNMENT\nSEC (Social/Enterprising/Conventional): S fits community engagement and support; E fits coordination and follow-through; C fits reliable programme records. Proposed role-environment orientation, not an individual assessment.\n\nAPTITUDE ALIGNMENT\nKey areas: Verbal Reasoning, Data Interpretation, Logical Reasoning\nReadiness: Convert community accounts into accurate registers, reconcile stakeholder needs and track project actions. These are draft importance estimates for role requirements, not learner aptitude scores; SME review required.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL ALIGNMENT\nKey traits: Agreeableness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion\nWork style: Clear communication and dependable follow-up support coordination without making unverified assurances. Trait-demand ratings are provisional role descriptors, not personality diagnosis or exclusion criteria.\n\nWORK-VALUE ALIGNMENT\nKey values: Impact, Leadership, Autonomy\nMotivation: Community impact and cooperative project work are central; accountability stays with the authorised project lead. Work-value ratings are provisional career-exploration descriptors, not employment or pay guarantees.\n\nEDUCATION AND ELIGIBILITY\nGate: Preferred\nStreams: B.Sc. Zoology; B.Sc. Botany; B.Sc. Geography; B.Sc. Geology; B.Sc. Agriculture; B.F.Sc. Fisheries Science; BBA; MBA Operations; MBA Finance; MBA Sustainability; BA Economics; BA Sociology; BA Social Work; BSW; MSW; BA Public Administration; BA Political Science; BA Psychology; B.Sc. Psychology; Public Health programmes\n"}'::jsonb;
